<FeatureLayout
  title="Git-Friendly Schema Format"
  description="The .pgd XML format produces clean single-line git diffs when you add or change columns. Turn two versions from git history into an ALTER migration with pgdesigner diff."
  subtitle="Review schema changes like code — clean diffs, meaningful context, migrations straight from git history."
>

<h2>The problem with other tools</h2>

<p>pgModeler keeps the whole model in one large XML file (split models are a paid Plus feature). DrawDB dumps everything into a single JSON blob. dbdiagram.io doesn't even give you a local file. Reviewing these diffs in a pull request is painful.</p>

<h2>One column = one line in the diff</h2>

<p>PgDesigner's <code>.pgd</code> format is XML designed specifically for version control. Each column is one line with all attributes inline, and tables are written in a stable order:</p>

<pre><code>&lt;schema name="public"&gt;
  &lt;table name="orders"&gt;
    &lt;column name="id" type="bigint"&gt;
      &lt;identity generated="by-default"&gt;&lt;/identity&gt;
    &lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;column name="user_id" type="bigint" nullable="false"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;column name="total" type="numeric" precision="10" scale="2" nullable="false"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;pk name="orders_pkey"&gt;
      &lt;column name="id"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;/pk&gt;
    &lt;fk name="fk_orders_user_id" to-table="users" on-delete="no action" on-update="no action"&gt;
      &lt;column name="user_id" references="id"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;/fk&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/schema&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Adding a column produces exactly one line in <code>git diff</code>:</p>

<pre><code>    &lt;column name="user_id" type="bigint" nullable="false"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
+   &lt;column name="status" type="varchar" length="20"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;
    &lt;column name="total" type="numeric" precision="10" scale="2" nullable="false"&gt;&lt;/column&gt;</code></pre>

<p>Closing tags like <code>&lt;/table&gt;</code> give context in diffs — you always know which table was changed, unlike JSON's anonymous closing braces.</p>

<h2>Why not HCL, DBML, or JSON?</h2>

<div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-6 mb-8">
  <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-alt border border-border-default p-4">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-heading mb-1">vs pgModeler XML</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">One large file per model; splitting it requires the paid Plus edition.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-alt border border-border-default p-4">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-heading mb-1">vs DrawDB JSON</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">Single JSON blob — entire file shows as changed. No meaningful context.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-alt border border-border-default p-4">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-heading mb-1">vs Atlas HCL</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">Clean diffs, but no visual editor. CLI-only workflow.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="rounded-lg bg-surface-alt border border-border-default p-4">
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-heading mb-1">vs dbdiagram DBML</h3>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">Text-based and git-friendly, but SaaS — no local files unless you export.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>From git history to a migration</h2>

<p>Because the model lives in git, any two commits are two schemas. Feed them to <code>pgdesigner diff</code> and you get the <code>ALTER</code> script between them, with hazard warnings:</p>

<pre><code>git show main:schema.pgd &gt; /tmp/main.pgd
pgdesigner diff /tmp/main.pgd schema.pgd -o migration.sql</code></pre>

<p>Run <code>pgdesigner lint schema.pgd</code> in the same pipeline — it exits with code <code>1</code> when the schema has errors.</p>

